            "content": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The arc of the program"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Toggle Headings unfold to reveal content."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "blk_fqpyxvxs8p",
          "type": "accordion",
          "v": 1,
          "column": 1,
          "fields": {
            "items": [
              {
                "question": "01 Why this matters Before we can build something together, we have to share a language for it. +",
                "answer": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Most people have heard the phrase \"tragedy of the commons\" — the idea that when something is shared, it inevitably gets used up. What fewer people know is that this was an argument made about "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "unmanaged open access",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": ", not about actual community-managed commons. And fewer still know that "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "real commons have thrived for centuries",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Elinor Ostrom won the Nobel Prize in economics for documenting this. The Commons Teach-Ins exist because "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "taking care of shared things together is one of the oldest, most successful human practices",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " — and most of us have forgotten how to do it. Recovering that knowledge is preparation for the work ahead."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              {
                "question": "02 What we cover We learn from the past and observe our now to co-create our shared future +",
                "answer": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "We move from a time when all peoples were "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "indigenous",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " to a place, through the centuries of fencing-off shared land into private property, through the cooperatives and movements that kept community-care alive, to the new local experiments emerging today."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "We draw on Ostrom's research, Indigenous traditions of stewardship, the long European history of shared land, and the practical wisdom of the communities that never stopped doing this work."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              {
                "question": "03 How it runs Once a month. Free. Anyone can come. +",
                "answer": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Sessions run once a month and are open to everyone — neighbors, students, organizers, curious passersby. There are no prerequisites. We bring strong coffee, good chairs, and a willingness to think out loud together."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Each session leaves something behind: a reading list, a recording, notes you can take with you. Over time these add up to a free, public curriculum and an eventual book — built openly, by the people who actually showed up."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              {
                "question": "04 What you leave with You leave with a different way of seeing. +",
                "answer": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Not certainty. Not a doctrine. "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "A vocabulary, a bit of history, and a sense of what's possible.",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " The Commons is a practice you'll start to recognize once you have a name for it — in your neighborhood, in your watershed, in your workplace. We're trying to make that recognition easier."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            ],
            "exclusive": false,
            "openFirst": false,
            "faqSchema": false
          }
        }
      ]
    },
